      "math_academy_id": "q-119189",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "A scientist experiments with bacteria. The number of bacteria $B(t)$ in his experiment increases at a rate proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment. Additionally, due to environmental conditions, $250$ bacteria die every hour.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the population of bacteria at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in hours?",
      "worked_solution": "The rate of change in the number of bacteria per hour increases in proportion to $B$ and decreases by $250.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=kB-250,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality.",
